1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,650
Chapter 37: Puerperal Infection.

3
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:26,850
Puerperal Pelvic Infections Uterine Infection Abdominal Incisional Infections Peritonitis And Adnexal Abscesses Parametrial Phlegmon Septic Pelvic Thrombophlebitis Perineal Infections Toxic Shock Syndrome Breast Infections Breast Abscess References Women are susceptible to several potentially serious complications during the fourth trimester.

4
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:33,025
Many of these conditions are encountered during pregnancy, and others are unique to the puerperium.

5
00:00:33,025 --> 00:00:42,825
Historically, infection was the most important source of postpartum maternal morbidity and mortality, since emphasized by the studies of Semmelweis and Lister .

6
00:00:42,825 --> 00:00:48,775
Puerperal infections include pelvic infections, mastitis, and breast abscesses.

7
00:00:48,775 --> 00:01:03,437
Discussed in their respective chapters, cardiovascular disease , venous thromboembolism , and hemorrhage currently are leading noninfectious puerperal complications (Callaghan, 2012; Creanga, 2017).

8
00:01:03,437 --> 00:01:08,925
However, the incidence of postpartum hospitalization due to sepsis is rising.

9
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:14,037
Other puerperal issues and their management are discussed in Chapter 36.

10
00:01:14,037 --> 00:01:16,687
Puerperal Pelvic Infections.

11
00:01:16,687 --> 00:01:24,175
Traditionally, the term puerperal infection describes any bacterial infection of the genital tract after delivery.

12
00:01:24,175 --> 00:01:33,137
Infection, preeclampsia, and obstetrical hemorrhage formed the lethal triad of maternal death causes before and during the 20th century.

13
00:01:33,137 --> 00:01:40,037
Fortunately, maternal mortality from puerperal infection is uncommon because of effective antibiotics.

14
00:01:40,037 --> 00:01:55,562
Creanga and associates (2017) reported results from the Pregnancy Mortality Surveillance System, which contained 4693 pregnancy-related maternal deaths in the United States from 2006 through 2010.

15
00:01:55,562 --> 00:02:01,712
Infection caused 13.6 percent of the deaths and was the second leading etiology.

16
00:02:01,712 --> 00:02:12,450
In an analysis of the North Carolina population, Berg and colleagues (2005) reported that 40 percent of infection-related maternal deaths were preventable.

17
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:14,875
# Puerperal Fever.

18
00:02:14,875 --> 00:02:25,800
Several infective and noninfective factors cause puerperal fever defined by a temperature of 38.0°C (100.4°F) or higher.

19
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:41,162
Using this conservative definition, Filker and Monif (1979) reported that only approximately 20 percent of women febrile within the first 24 hours after vaginal delivery were subsequently diagnosed with pelvic infection.

20
00:02:41,162 --> 00:02:45,900
This value was 70 percent in those undergoing cesarean delivery.

21
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:51,300
Most persistent fevers after childbirth are caused by genital tract infection.

22
00:02:51,300 --> 00:03:05,362
Of note, spiking fevers ≥39°C within the first 24 hours postpartum may be associated with virulent pelvic infection caused by group A streptococcus (p. 651).

23
00:03:05,362 --> 00:03:18,000
Other sources of puerperal fever include breast engorgement, urinary infections, episiotomy and abdominal incisions, perineal lacerations, and postcesarean respiratory complications.

24
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,225
Approximately 15 percent of women who do not breastfeed develop fever from breast engorgement.

25
00:03:24,225 --> 00:03:32,862
Breast fever” rarely exceeds 39°C in the first few postpartum days and usually lasts <24 hours.

26
00:03:32,862 --> 00:03:36,587
The incidence of fever is lower in breastfeeding women .

27
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:42,025
Postpartum urinary infections are uncommon because of the normal diuresis.

28
00:03:42,025 --> 00:03:46,100
Acute pyelonephritis has a variable clinical picture.

29
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:54,275
The first sign of renal infection may be fever, followed later by costovertebral angle tenderness, nausea, and vomiting.

30
00:03:54,275 --> 00:04:05,312
Atelectasis following general anesthesia for cesarean delivery is caused by hypoventilation and is best prevented by coughing and deep breathing on a fixed schedule following surgery.

31
00:04:05,312 --> 00:04:12,987
Fever with atelectasis is due to infection triggered by proliferation of normal flora distal to obstructing mucus plugs.

32
00:04:12,987 --> 00:04:15,600
# Uterine Infection.

33
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:22,862
Postpartum uterine infection has been called endometritis, endomyometritis, and endoparametritis.

34
00:04:22,862 --> 00:04:30,850
Following vaginal delivery, infection involves not only the decidua but also the myometrium and parametrial tissues.

35
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:38,200
Infection after cesarean delivery is essentially a surgical site infection involving the incised myometrium.

36
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:43,737
In either case, we prefer the inclusive term metritis with pelvic cellulitis.

37
00:04:43,737 --> 00:04:50,200
Predisposing Factors Route of delivery is the single most significant risk factor for the development

38
00:04:50,356 --> 00:04:56,343
of uterine infection (Boggess, 2017; Moulton, 2018).

39
00:04:56,293 --> 00:05:09,618
In women undergoing cesarean delivery, rehospitalization rates for wound complications and metritis are higher than those with a vaginal birth (Axelsson, 2018; Fein, 2019).

40
00:05:09,618 --> 00:05:22,256
In the French Confidential Enquiry on Maternal Deaths, Deneux-Tharaux and coworkers (2006) cited a nearly 25fold greater infection-related mortality rate with cesarean delivery.

41
00:05:22,256 --> 00:05:29,356
With vaginal delivery, women delivered at Parkland Hospital have a 1to 2-percent incidence of metritis.

42
00:05:29,356 --> 00:05:37,806
This rate rises to 5 to 6 percent in those with ruptured membranes, prolonged labor, and multiple cervical examinations.

43
00:05:37,806 --> 00:05:49,581
If intrapartum chorioamnionitis is present, the risk of persistent uterine infection has exceeded 13 percent (DeNoble, 2019; Maberry, 1991).

44
00:05:49,581 --> 00:06:01,868
These figures are similar to those reported by the Maternal Fetal Medicine Units Network from a cohort of more than 115,000 women in whom the overall pelvic infection rate approximated 5 percent .

45
00:06:01,868 --> 00:06:09,906
With operative vaginal delivery, women carry higher risks for metritis and perineal infections than spontaneous birth .

46
00:06:09,906 --> 00:06:17,356
Notably, these deliveries are often implemented in the context of prolonged labor, which is a known metritis risk.

47
00:06:17,356 --> 00:06:26,781
One study of 3500 such women showed that those receiving a single dose of prophylactic antibiotics had a lower maternal infection rate .

48
00:06:26,781 --> 00:06:32,343
Infections include those of the urinary tract, uterus, or perineal wound.

49
00:06:32,343 --> 00:06:41,931
Labor lengths were not presented, but in approximately 50 percent of participants, “failure to progress” was the operative vaginal delivery indication.

50
00:06:41,931 --> 00:06:48,856
Moreover, 10 percent of women had rupture of membranes lengths between 24 and 48 hours.

51
00:06:48,856 --> 00:07:00,468
The contribution of these, rather than the delivery mode, will require further investigation before this prophylaxis is widely adopted solely for the indication of operative vaginal delivery.

52
00:07:00,468 --> 00:07:06,681
With cesarean delivery, hysterotomy is associated with significant infectious morbidity.

53
00:07:06,681 --> 00:07:14,331
In the 1970s, pelvic infection complicated 50 percent of cesarean deliveries at Parkland Hospital.

54
00:07:14,331 --> 00:07:25,381
Fortunately, antimicrobial prophylaxis has done more to decrease the incidence and severity of postcesarean infections than any other intervention in the past 30 years.

55
00:07:25,381 --> 00:07:32,756
Thus, a single dose of perioperative antibiotics is recommended for all women undergoing cesarean delivery .

56
00:07:32,756 --> 00:07:39,306
Such practices lower the puerperal pelvic infection risk by 65 to 75 percent .

57
00:07:39,306 --> 00:07:49,093
β-lactam antimicrobials are superior to other agents, and elements of this prophylaxis are outlined in Chapter 30 (p. 551) .

58
00:07:49,093 --> 00:07:59,793
Important risk factors for infection following surgery include prolonged labor, membrane rupture, multiple cervical examinations, and internal fetal monitoring.

59
00:07:59,793 --> 00:08:07,706
Regardless of delivery route, pelvic infection generally is more frequent in women of lower socioeconomic status .

60
00:08:07,706 --> 00:08:16,593
Except in extreme cases usually not seen in developed countries, it is uncommon that anemia or poor nutrition predispose to infection.

61
00:08:16,593 --> 00:08:40,881
Bacterial colonization of the lower genital tract with certain microorganisms—for example, group B streptococcus, Chlamydia trachomatis, Mycoplasma hominis, Ureaplasma urealyticum, and Gardnerella vaginalis—has been associated with an increased postpartum infection risk (Andrews, 1995; Jacobsson, 2002; Watts, 1990).

62
00:08:40,881 --> 00:09:05,331
Other factors associated with a greater risk include general anesthesia, significant hysterotomy extension, young maternal age, nulliparity, prolonged labor induction, chorioamnionitis, obesity, and meconium-stained amnionic fluid (Acosta, 2012; Leth, 2011; Patel, 2019; Siriwachirachai, 2014).

63
00:09:05,331 --> 00:09:08,418
Microbiology Common Pathogens.

64
00:09:08,418 --> 00:09:16,243
Most pelvic infections are caused by bacteria indigenous to the genital tract and listed in Table 37-1.

65
00:09:16,243 --> 00:09:21,818
Most of these infections are polymicrobial, which enhances bacterial synergy.

66
00:09:21,818 --> 00:09:26,918
Other factors that promote virulence are hematomas and devitalized tissue.

67
00:09:26,918 --> 00:09:35,718
Although the cervix and vagina routinely harbor bacteria, the uterine cavity is usually sterile before rupture of the amnionic sac.

68
00:09:35,718 --> 00:09:44,418
As the consequence of labor and delivery and associated manipulations, anaerobic and aerobic bacteria contaminate the amnionic

69
00:09:44,572 --> 00:09:46,697
fluid and uterus.

70
00:09:46,647 --> 00:09:49,234
Table 37-1.

71
00:09:49,234 --> 00:10:07,222
Bacteria Commonly Responsible for Female Genital Infections Prior to the routine use of antimicrobial prophylaxis, Gilstrap and Cunningham (1979) cultured amnionic fluid obtained at cesarean delivery from women in labor with membranes ruptured more than 6 hours.

72
00:10:07,222 --> 00:10:14,084
All had bacterial growth, and an average of 2.5 organisms was identified from each specimen.

73
00:10:14,084 --> 00:10:23,547
Anaerobic and aerobic organisms were found in 63 percent, anaerobes alone in 30 percent, and aerobes alone in only 7 percent.

74
00:10:23,547 --> 00:10:34,272
Anaerobes included Peptostreptococcus and Peptococcus species in 45 percent, Bacteroides species in 9 percent, and Clostridium species in 3 percent.

75
00:10:34,272 --> 00:10:41,009
Clostridial species rarely cause puerperal infections, but infections can be severe in those cases .

76
00:10:41,009 --> 00:10:50,584
Aerobes included Enterococcus in 14 percent, group B Streptococcus in 8 percent, and Escherichia coli in 9 percent of isolates.

77
00:10:50,584 --> 00:11:01,722
Sherman and associates (1999) later showed that bacterial isolates at cesarean delivery correlated with those taken from women with metritis at 3 days postpartum.

78
00:11:01,722 --> 00:11:13,447
Group B streptococci, E coli, and enterococci are some of the more common blood culture isolates with metritis (Cape, 2013; O’Higgins, 2014).

79
00:11:13,447 --> 00:11:23,247
In the past 15 years, skin and soft-tissue infections due to methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) have become prevalent .

80
00:11:23,247 --> 00:11:36,284
However, MRSA is more commonly implicated in abdominal and perineal incisional infections and less often in puerperal metritis (Anderson, 2007; Patel, 2007).

81
00:11:36,284 --> 00:11:42,847
Group A β-hemolytic streptococcal infections have a reported incidence of 1 in 1220 births .

82
00:11:42,847 --> 00:11:57,909
During the past 25 years, group A streptococcus has been reported to cause a toxic shock–like syndrome and life-threatening infection (Donders, 2021; Gustafson, 2017; Shinar, 2016).

83
00:11:57,909 --> 00:12:09,247
In reviews by Crum (2002) and Udagawa (1999), group A streptococcal infections manifested before, during, or within 12 hours of delivery.

84
00:12:09,247 --> 00:12:17,272
Affected women had a maternal mortality rate of almost 90 percent, and the fetal mortality rate exceeded 50 percent.

85
00:12:17,272 --> 00:12:22,409
The role of other organisms in the etiology of these infections is unclear.

86
00:12:22,409 --> 00:12:32,209
Observations of Chaim and coworkers (2003) suggest that heavy cervical colonization of U urealyticum may contribute to metritis development.

87
00:12:32,209 --> 00:12:49,922
To counter this potential pathogen, azithromycin-based extended-spectrum antibiotic prophylaxis reduces postoperative cesarean delivery infections from 12 to 6 percent compared with β-lactam agents alone (Harper, 2017; Tita, 2016).

88
00:12:49,922 --> 00:12:55,297
Chlamydial infections have been implicated in late-onset, indolent metritis .

89
00:12:55,297 --> 00:13:07,784
Last, Jacobsson and colleagues (2002) reported a threefold higher risk of puerperal infection in a group of Swedish women in whom bacterial vaginosis was identified in early pregnancy .

90
00:13:07,784 --> 00:13:10,034
Bacterial Cultures.

91
00:13:10,034 --> 00:13:17,222
Routine genital tract cultures obtained before treatment serve little clinical use and add significant costs.

92
00:13:17,222 --> 00:13:23,084
Women with a temperature 102°F are more likely to have bacteremia .

93
00:13:23,084 --> 00:13:27,297
Even so, routine blood cultures seldom modify care.

94
00:13:27,297 --> 00:13:45,822
In two studies done before perioperative prophylaxis was used, blood cultures were positive in 13 percent of women with postcesarean metritis at Parkland Hospital and in 24 percent of those at Los Angeles County Hospital (Cunningham, 1978; DiZerega, 1979).

95
00:13:45,822 --> 00:13:52,872
In a later Finnish study, bacteremia was identified in only 5 percent of women with puerperal sepsis .

96
00:13:52,872 --> 00:14:00,897
Exceptions might be women with exceedingly high temperature spikes that may signify virulent infection with group A streptococci .

97
00:14:00,897 --> 00:14:14,184
Pathogenesis and Clinical Course Puerperal infection following vaginal delivery primarily involves the placental implantation site, decidua and adjacent myometrium, or cervicovaginal lacerations.

98
00:14:14,184 --> 00:14:21,159
The pathogenesis of uterine infection following cesarean delivery is that of an infected surgical incision.

99
00:14:21,159 --> 00:14:27,084
Bacteria that colonize the cervix and vagina gain access to the uterus during labor.

100
00:14:27,084 --> 00:14:31,072
Postpartum, they invade devitalized uterine tissue.

101
00:14:31,072 --> 00:14:38,284
Parametrial cellulitis follows with infection of the pelvic retroperitoneal fibroareolar connective tissue.

102
00:14:38,284 --> 00:14:42,084
With early treatment, infection is contained within the

103
00:14:42,244 --> 00:14:47,569
parametrial and paravaginal tissue, but it may extend deep into the pelvis.

104
00:14:47,519 --> 00:14:53,081
Fever is the most important criterion for the diagnosis of postpartum metritis.

105
00:14:53,081 --> 00:14:59,381
Intuitively, the degree of fever is believed proportional to the extent of infection and sepsis.

106
00:14:59,381 --> 00:15:13,844
Temperatures usually are 38 to 39°C, and temperatures >39°C suggest bacteremia or endotoxemia (Easter, 2017; Suffredini, 1989).

107
00:15:13,844 --> 00:15:21,931
Women usually complain of abdominal pain, and parametrial tenderness is elicited on abdominal and bimanual examination.

108
00:15:21,931 --> 00:15:31,856
Leukocytosis may range from 15,000 to 30,000 cells/μL, but recall that cesarean delivery itself raises the leukocyte count.

109
00:15:31,856 --> 00:15:39,831
Although an offensive odor can develop, many women have foul-smelling lochia without evidence for infection, and vice versa.

110
00:15:39,831 --> 00:15:48,706
Some other infections, notably those caused by group A β-hemolytic streptococci, may be associated with scant, odorless lochia .

111
00:15:48,706 --> 00:15:57,706
Treatment If metritis develops following vaginal delivery, treatment with an oral or intramuscular antimicrobial agent may be sufficient .

112
00:15:57,706 --> 00:16:05,769
For moderate to severe infections, however, intravenous therapy with a broad-spectrum antibiotic regimen is indicated.

113
00:16:05,769 --> 00:16:14,244
Improvement occurs in 48 to 72 hours in nearly 90 percent of women treated with one of several regimens discussed below.

114
00:16:14,244 --> 00:16:20,906
Persistent fever after this interval mandates a careful search for causes of refractory pelvic infection.

115
00:16:20,906 --> 00:16:32,569
These include a parametrial phlegmon—an area of intense cellulitis; an abdominal incisional or pelvic abscess or infected hematoma; and septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.

116
00:16:32,569 --> 00:16:40,544
In our experience, persistent fever is seldom due to antimicrobial-resistant bacteria or due to drug side effects.

117
00:16:40,544 --> 00:16:49,656
The woman may be discharged home after she has been afebrile for at least 24 hours, and further oral antimicrobial therapy is not needed .

118
00:16:49,656 --> 00:16:52,219
Choice of Antimicrobials.

119
00:16:52,219 --> 00:16:59,456
Therapy is empirical and initial treatment is directed against the mixed flora shown in Table 37-1.

120
00:16:59,456 --> 00:17:08,294
For infections following vaginal delivery, as many as 90 percent of women respond to regimens such as ampicillin plus gentamicin.

121
00:17:08,294 --> 00:17:14,431
In contrast, anaerobic coverage is included for infections following cesarean delivery .

122
00:17:14,431 --> 00:17:17,031
Table 37-2.

123
00:17:17,031 --> 00:17:34,719
Antimicrobial Regimens for Pelvic Infections Following Cesarean Delivery In 1979, DiZerega and coworkers compared the effectiveness of clindamycin plus gentamicin with that of penicillin G plus gentamicin for treatment of pelvic infections following cesarean delivery.

124
00:17:34,719 --> 00:17:45,081
Women given the clindamycin-gentamicin regimen had a 95-percent response rate, and this regimen is still considered by most to be the standard by which others are measured .

125
00:17:45,081 --> 00:17:50,819
Despite this standard therapy, enterococcal cultures may be persistently positive.

126
00:17:50,819 --> 00:17:59,294
The addition of ampicillin, either initially or following no response after 48 to 72 hours, targets enterococci .

127
00:17:59,294 --> 00:18:04,794
Many authorities recommend periodical monitoring of serum gentamicin levels.

128
00:18:04,794 --> 00:18:14,044
At Parkland Hospital, we do not routinely do so if a woman has adequate renal function, which is evidenced by a normal serum creatinine level.

129
00:18:14,044 --> 00:18:22,544
Once-daily dosing versus multiple dosing with gentamicin provides adequate serum levels, and either method has similar cure rates .

130
00:18:22,544 --> 00:18:35,431
In the event of diminished glomerular filtration, some recommend a combination of clindamycin and a second-generation cephalosporin, because of potential nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity with gentamicin.

131
00:18:35,431 --> 00:18:45,656
Instead, others recommend a combination of clindamycin and aztreonam—the latter is a monobactam compound with activity similar to the aminoglycosides.

132
00:18:45,656 --> 00:18:52,256
The spectra of β-lactam antimicrobials include activity against many anaerobic pathogens.

133
00:18:52,256 --> 00:18:59,944
Some examples include cephalosporins such as cefoxitin, cefotetan, cefotaxime, and ceftriaxone.

134
00:18:59,944 --> 00:19:05,744
Extended-spectrum penicillins are piperacillin, ticarcillin, and mezlocillin.

135
00:19:05,744 --> 00:19:13,819
β-lactam antimicrobials are inherently safe and, except for allergic reactions, are free of major toxicity.

136
00:19:13,819 --> 00:19:25,694
The β-lactamase inhibitors clavulanic acid, sulbactam, and tazobactam have been combined with ampicillin, amoxicillin, ticarcillin, and piperacillin to extend their spectra.

137
00:19:25,694 --> 00:19:29,544
Metronidazole has superior in vitro activity

138
00:19:29,692 --> 00:19:32,079
against most anaerobes.

139
00:19:32,029 --> 00:19:41,017
This agent given with ampicillin and an aminoglycoside provides coverage against most organisms encountered in serious pelvic infections.

140
00:19:41,017 --> 00:19:46,892
It is also used second line to treat some cases of Clostridioides difficile colitis.

141
00:19:46,892 --> 00:19:51,754
Imipenem and similar antimicrobials are in the carbapenem family.

142
00:19:51,754 --> 00:19:57,567
These offer broad-spectrum coverage against most organisms associated with metritis.

143
00:19:57,567 --> 00:20:03,179
Imipenem coupled with cilastatin inhibits the antibiotic’s renal metabolism.

144
00:20:03,179 --> 00:20:13,217
Because of imipenem’s higher cost, it is reasonable from both a medical and an economic standpoint to reserve this drug for serious nonobstetrical infections.

145
00:20:13,217 --> 00:20:19,379
Vancomycin is a glycopeptide antimicrobial active against gram-positive bacteria.

146
00:20:19,379 --> 00:20:30,254
It is used in lieu of β-lactam therapy for a patient with a type 1 allergic reaction and given for suspected infections due to S aureus and to treat C difficile colitis .

147
00:20:30,254 --> 00:20:37,679
Perioperative Prophylaxis The use of periprocedural techniques for infection prevention is common in obstetrics .

148
00:20:37,679 --> 00:20:50,554
Numerous studies show that prophylactic antibiotics at the time of cesarean delivery reduce wound and postpartum pelvic infection rates (Carter, 2017; Smaill, 2014).

149
00:20:50,554 --> 00:20:55,892
The observed benefit applies to both elective and nonelective cesarean delivery.

150
00:20:55,892 --> 00:21:05,242
As noted from preliminary data, antimicrobials may decrease pelvic infection rates after operative vaginal delivery (p. 650).

151
00:21:05,242 --> 00:21:22,867
However, data are insufficient to suggest prophylactic antimicrobials lower infection rates after spontaneous vaginal delivery, repair of all episiotomies, or manual extraction of the placenta (Bonet 2017a,b; Chongsomchai, 2014).

152
00:21:22,867 --> 00:21:36,004
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) concluded that a single antibiotic dose with third- and fourth-degree perineal laceration is reasonable and has evidenced-based support.

153
00:21:36,004 --> 00:21:38,554
Table 37-3.

154
00:21:38,554 --> 00:21:49,942
Various Prophylactic Methods for Decreasing Pelvic and Wound Infection Rates Following Delivery Single-dose prophylaxis with a 2-g dose of a first-generation cephalosporin is ideal.

155
00:21:49,942 --> 00:21:55,804
This regimen has similar efficacy of broad-spectrum agents or multiple-dose regimens .

156
00:21:55,804 --> 00:22:02,842
For obese women, evidence supports a 3-g dose of cefazolin to reach optimal tissue concentrations .

157
00:22:02,842 --> 00:22:15,017
Some evidence supports addition of azithromycin to lower postcesarean uterine infection rates (Markwei, 2021; Pierce, 2021; Tita, 2016).

158
00:22:15,017 --> 00:22:21,017
Women colonized with MRSA are given vancomycin in addition to a cephalosporin .

159
00:22:21,017 --> 00:22:38,854
It is controversial whether the infection rate is reduced further if the antimicrobial is given before the skin incision compared with after umbilical cord clamping (Baaqeel, 2013; Macones, 2012; Sun, 2013; Ward, 2016).

160
00:22:38,854 --> 00:22:48,117
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) has concluded that the evidence favors predelivery administration.

161
00:22:48,117 --> 00:22:54,017
Most women with a stated allergy to penicillin are not prone to developing anaphylaxis.

162
00:22:54,017 --> 00:23:00,304
Without a history of anaphylaxis, most of these women can safely be given a cephalosporin .

163
00:23:00,304 --> 00:23:05,642
If not, then vancomycin is given along with clindamycin and gentamicin .

164
00:23:05,642 --> 00:23:11,754
Preoperative abdominal skin preparation decreases the risk for pelvic and wound infections .

165
00:23:11,754 --> 00:23:19,229
Skin preparation with chlorhexidinealcohol is superior to iodine-alcohol for preventing surgical-site infections .

166
00:23:19,229 --> 00:23:34,092
Additive beneficial effects may be gained by preoperative vaginal cleansing with povidone-iodine rinse or application of metronidazole gel (Caissutti, 2017; Felder, 2019; Haas, 2018).

167
00:23:34,092 --> 00:23:36,779
Other Methods of Prophylaxis.

168
00:23:36,779 --> 00:23:42,067
Several studies have addressed the value of prenatal cervicovaginal cultures.

169
00:23:42,067 --> 00:23:52,617
These are obtained in the hope of identifying pathogens that might be eradicated to lower incidences of preterm labor, chorioamnionitis, and puerperal infections.

170
00:23:52,617 --> 00:23:59,242
Unfortunately, treatment of asymptomatic vaginal infections does not prevent these complications.

171
00:23:59,242 --> 00:24:07,904
For asymptomatic bacterial vaginosis, Carey and associates (2000) reported no beneficial effects for women treated.

172
00:24:07,904 --> 00:24:18,579
For asymptomatic Trichomonas vaginalis infection, a similar postpartum infection rate was found in women treated in the second trimester compared with placebo-treated women .

173
00:24:18,579 --> 00:24:21,079
Technical maneuvers done to

174
00:24:21,244 --> 00:24:25,131
alter the postcesarean infection rate have been studied .

175
00:24:25,081 --> 00:24:38,031
Allowing the placenta to separate spontaneously and exteriorizing the uterus to close the hysterotomy may reduce the infection risk (Jacobs-Jokhan, 2004; Lasley, 1997).

176
00:24:38,031 --> 00:24:54,569
However, changing gloves after placental delivery, cleaning the intrauterine cavity, and dilating the lower segment and cervix do not alter the infection rate (Atkinson, 1996; Eke, 2019; Liabsuetrakul, 2018).

177
00:24:54,569 --> 00:25:01,494
No differences were found in postoperative infection rates when single- and two-layer uterine closures were compared .

178
00:25:01,494 --> 00:25:11,856
Similarly, infection rates are not affected by closure versus nonclosure of the peritoneum (Bamigboye, 2014; Tulandi, 2003).

179
00:25:11,856 --> 00:25:21,456
Importantly, closure of subcutaneous tissue in obese women does not lower the wound infection rate, but it does decrease the wound separation incidence .

180
00:25:21,456 --> 00:25:32,556
Similarly, skin closure with staples versus suture has a greater incidence of noninfectious skin separation (Mackeen, 2012; Tuuli, 2011).

181
00:25:32,556 --> 00:25:36,806
# Complications of Uterine and Pelvic Infections.

182
00:25:36,806 --> 00:25:44,481
Metritis responds to antimicrobial treatment within 48 to 72 hours in more than 90 percent of women.

183
00:25:44,481 --> 00:25:49,131
In some of the remainder, any of several complications may arise.

184
00:25:49,131 --> 00:26:01,619
These include wound infection, complex pelvic infection such as a phlegmon or an abscess, and septic pelvic thrombophlebitis (Brown, 1999; Jaiyeoba, 2012).

185
00:26:01,619 --> 00:26:11,981
As with other aspects of puerperal infections, the incidence and severity of these complications are reduced by perioperative antimicrobial prophylaxis.

186
00:26:11,981 --> 00:26:15,444
# Abdominal Incisional Infections.

187
00:26:15,444 --> 00:26:21,081
Wound infection is a common cause of persistent fever in women treated for metritis.

188
00:26:21,081 --> 00:26:33,619
Incisional infection risk factors include obesity, diabetes, corticosteroid therapy, immunosuppression, anemia, hypertension, and hematoma formation from inadequate hemostasis.

189
00:26:33,619 --> 00:26:46,969
If prophylactic antimicrobials are given, the incidence of wound infection following cesarean delivery ranges from 2 to 10 percent depending on risk factors (Andrews, 2003; Chaim, 2000).

190
00:26:46,969 --> 00:26:55,419
From our experiences at Parkland Hospital, the incidence is closer to 2 percent, but this risk rises with increasing body mass .

191
00:26:55,419 --> 00:27:04,544
Incisional abscesses that develop following cesarean delivery usually cause persistent fever or fever that begins on approximately the fourth day.

192
00:27:04,544 --> 00:27:07,944
The wound is erythematous and drains pus.

193
00:27:07,944 --> 00:27:19,669
Organisms that cause wound infections are generally the same as those isolated from amnionic fluid at cesarean delivery, however, hospital-acquired pathogens also may be causative.

194
00:27:19,669 --> 00:27:25,994
Treatment includes antimicrobials and surgical drainage and debridement of devitalized tissue.

195
00:27:25,994 --> 00:27:30,806
This typically requires spinal analgesia or general anesthesia.

196
00:27:30,806 --> 00:27:34,956
The fascia is carefully inspected to document integrity.

197
00:27:34,956 --> 00:27:38,894
Local wound care thereafter is completed twice daily.

198
00:27:38,894 --> 00:27:49,731
Before each dressing change, procedural analgesia is tailored to wound size and location, and oral, intramuscular, or intravenous dosage routes are suitable.

199
00:27:49,731 --> 00:27:52,906
Topical lidocaine also may be added.

200
00:27:52,906 --> 00:27:57,894
Necrotic tissue is removed, and the wound is repacked with moist gauze.

201
00:27:57,894 --> 00:28:07,419
At 4 to 6 days, healthy granulation tissue is typically present, and secondary en bloc closure of the open layers can usually be accomplished .

202
00:28:07,419 --> 00:28:16,669
As shown in Figure 37-1, a polypropylene or nylon suture of appropriate gauge enters 2 to 3 cm from one wound edge.

203
00:28:16,669 --> 00:28:23,706
It crosses the wound to incorporate the full wound thickness and emerges 3 cm from the other wound edge.

204
00:28:23,706 --> 00:28:27,094
These are placed in series to close the opening.

205
00:28:27,094 --> 00:28:32,119
In most cases, sutures may be removed on postprocedural day 10.

206
00:28:32,119 --> 00:28:34,769
Figure 37-1.

207
00:28:34,769 --> 00:28:38,294
Secondary abdominal wound closure technique.

208
00:28:38,294 --> 00:28:43,606
Reproduced with permission from Worley KC: Postoperative complications.

209
00:28:43,606 --> 00:28:53,119
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

210
00:28:53,119 --> 00:28:57,606
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017).

211
00:28:57,606 --> 00:29:03,844
Vacuum-Assisted Wound Closure This system promotes healing by applying negative pressure to the wound .

212
00:29:03,844 --> 00:29:14,806
The technique is variably referred to as vacuum-assisted closure (VAC), topical negative pressure (TNP), and negative-pressure wound therapy (NPWT).

213
00:29:14,806 --> 00:29:18,881
Negative-pressure systems are used for open wounds after

214
00:29:19,036 --> 00:29:23,161
infection or for prophylaxis against wound disruption.

215
00:29:23,111 --> 00:29:35,586
Mouës and colleagues (2011) confirmed that NPWT enhances blood flow to the wound, promotes angiogenesis, induces cellular proliferation, and shrinks wound size.

216
00:29:35,586 --> 00:29:42,573
Several systems are available and widely accepted, despite meager formal evidence for clinical efficacy.

217
00:29:42,573 --> 00:29:45,148
Figure 37-2.

218
00:29:45,148 --> 00:29:55,448
Theoretical effects of negative-pressure wound therapy include macro- and microdeformation, removal of tissue fluid, and creation of a warm and moist environment.

219
00:29:55,448 --> 00:30:00,298
As shown in the inset, tissue fluid is drawn out by suction tubing.

220
00:30:00,298 --> 00:30:06,473
It travels through the porous sponge dressing that fills the wound and into an adjacent collection canister.

221
00:30:06,473 --> 00:30:13,236
As healing progresses, a layer of granulation tissue (red) forms at the wound-sponge interface.

222
00:30:13,236 --> 00:30:18,298
Reproduced with permission from Cunningham FG: Surgical instruments.

223
00:30:18,298 --> 00:30:27,811
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

224
00:30:27,811 --> 00:30:32,298
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

225
00:30:32,298 --> 00:30:40,411
Open abdominal wounds once infection has cleared or an “open surgical abdomen” is a major indication for NPWT.

226
00:30:40,411 --> 00:30:51,611
Vacuum therapy is the most efficient method of temporary abdominal closure for patients with open abdominal wounds (Bruhin, 2014; Quyn, 2012).

227
00:30:51,611 --> 00:30:59,311
However, no trials have compared vacuum-assisted wound closure with conventional wound care after cesarean delivery.

228
00:30:59,311 --> 00:31:08,236
Also, these devices are used for closure of perineal wounds resulting from infected episiotomies, hematomas, or abscesses .

229
00:31:08,236 --> 00:31:18,511
Very few randomized trials have compared vacuum-assisted wound closure with conventional wound care (Kawakita, 2021; Yu, 2018).

230
00:31:18,511 --> 00:31:20,436
Prophylaxis.

231
00:31:20,436 --> 00:31:28,136
Negative-pressure devices are also marketed to prevent wound infections in incisions closed to heal by primary intention.

232
00:31:28,136 --> 00:31:37,361
Such prophylactic use in 441 obese women undergoing cesarean delivery has been evaluated in two large randomized trials.

233
00:31:37,361 --> 00:31:48,211
Hussamy and associates (2019) found in obese women that the incidence of wound morbidity was similar in the standard dressing group compared with the NPWT group .

234
00:31:48,211 --> 00:31:54,261
The second trial was a multicenter study from Denmark of 876 obese women .

235
00:31:54,261 --> 00:32:04,573
In the NPWT group, the surgical site infection rate was 4.6 percent compared with 9.2 percent in the group of women treated with standard dressing.

236
00:32:04,573 --> 00:32:11,836
Similarly, a systematic review found use of prophylactic NPWT reduced wound complications .

237
00:32:11,836 --> 00:32:20,898
The cost effectiveness of these systems is inconclusive in multiple studies (Echebiri, 2015; Lewis, 2014).

238
00:32:20,898 --> 00:32:26,936
Smid and coworkers (2017) questioned the effectiveness of such therapy.

239
00:32:26,936 --> 00:32:38,298
Because of these uncertainties, we agree with Tuuli (2019) that routine use of prophylactic NPWT needs more evaluation before its widespread acceptance.

240
00:32:38,298 --> 00:32:40,898
Table 37-4.

241
00:32:40,898 --> 00:32:59,686
Randomized Trial of Prophylactic Incisional NegativePressure Wound Therapy (iNPWT) Versus Standard Surgical Dressing for Morbidly Obese Women Undergoing Cesarean Delivery Fascial Dehiscence This separation of the fascial layer is a serious complication, and bowel evisceration can be comorbid.

242
00:32:59,686 --> 00:33:07,986
Wound infection and obesity are prominent risk factors (Poole, 1985; Subramaniam, 2014).

243
00:33:07,986 --> 00:33:20,636
For example, McNeeley and associates (1998) reported a fascial dehiscence rate of approximately 1 per 300 operations in almost 9000 women undergoing cesarean delivery.

244
00:33:20,636 --> 00:33:28,661
Two thirds of the 27 fascial dehiscences in this study were associated with concurrent fascial infection and tissue necrosis.

245
00:33:28,661 --> 00:33:34,211
Fascial dehiscence generally presents within the first 7 to 10 postoperative days.

246
00:33:34,211 --> 00:33:42,361
Superficial disruption of the subcutaneous layer and extensive leakage of peritoneal fluid or purulent drainage are indicative.

247
00:33:42,361 --> 00:33:49,011
In unclear cases, CT scanning may be elected preoperatively, if obtained expeditiously.

248
00:33:49,011 --> 00:33:51,986
Dehiscence is a surgical emergency.

249
00:33:51,986 --> 00:34:01,248
If abdominal contents have eviscerated, sterile towels or gauze soaked in saline can be used early to cover and gently replace bowel or omentum.

250
00:34:01,248 --> 00:34:07,323
Broad-spectrum antibiotics are generally recommended to minimize ensuing peritonitis.

251
00:34:07,323 --> 00:34:17,286
Given the high mortality risk associated with fascial dehiscence and bowel evisceration, examination under anesthesia to estimate the extent of separation

252
00:34:17,452 --> 00:34:19,564
is often warranted.

253
00:34:19,514 --> 00:34:26,827
Surgery aims to assess bowel health, debride necrotic wound tissue, and close the fascia, if possible.

254
00:34:26,827 --> 00:34:33,639
For closure, an interrupted mass closure using a no. 2 permanent suture is recommended typically .

255
00:34:33,639 --> 00:34:39,402
In cases with infection, the subcutaneous layers are left to close secondarily.

256
00:34:39,402 --> 00:34:46,327
General surgery consultation is considered if bowel ischemia or difficult fascial closure is anticipated.

257
00:34:46,327 --> 00:34:48,952
Figure 37-3.

258
00:34:48,952 --> 00:35:00,089
Mass closure: each stitch is placed 1.5 to 2 cm from the wound edge (A) and incorporates the peritoneum, rectus muscle, and rectus sheath (B).

259
00:35:00,089 --> 00:35:04,927
Stitches are spaced 1 cm apart along the length of the incision.

260
00:35:04,927 --> 00:35:13,239
A.R.S. = anterior rectus sheath; P.R.S. = posterior rectus sheath; SubQ = subcutaneous layer.

261
00:35:13,239 --> 00:35:18,514
Reproduced with permission from Cundiff GW: Incisions and closures.

262
00:35:18,514 --> 00:35:28,027
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

263
00:35:28,027 --> 00:35:32,514
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

264
00:35:32,514 --> 00:35:35,652
# Necrotizing Fasciitis.

265
00:35:35,652 --> 00:35:40,989
This uncommon severe wound infection is associated with high mortality rates.

266
00:35:40,989 --> 00:35:50,602
In obstetrics, necrotizing fasciitis may involve abdominal incisions, or it may complicate episiotomy or other perineal lacerations.

267
00:35:50,602 --> 00:35:54,677
As the name implies, tissue necrosis is significant.

268
00:35:54,677 --> 00:36:05,877
Of the risk factors for fasciitis summarized by Owen and Andrews (1994), diabetes, obesity, and hypertension are increasingly common in gravidas.

269
00:36:05,877 --> 00:36:14,527
Like pelvic infections, this wound complication usually is polymicrobial and caused by organisms that make up the normal vaginal flora.

270
00:36:14,527 --> 00:36:27,502
In some cases, however, infection is caused by a single virulent bacterial species such as group A β-hemolytic streptococcus (Anderson, 2014; Rimawi, 2012).

271
00:36:27,502 --> 00:36:36,527
Occasionally, rarely encountered pathogens cause necrotizing infections (Chong, 2016; Swartz, 2004).

272
00:36:36,527 --> 00:36:45,827
Goepfert and coworkers (1997) described nine cases of necrotizing fasciitis in more than 5000 cesarean deliveries.

273
00:36:45,827 --> 00:36:52,202
The infection was fatal in two women—one with metastatic breast cancer and the other with sepsis.

274
00:36:52,202 --> 00:37:00,652
In another report, Schorge and colleagues (1998) described five women with fasciitis following cesarean delivery.

275
00:37:00,652 --> 00:37:05,364
None of these women had predisposing risk factors, and none died.

276
00:37:05,364 --> 00:37:13,577
Infection may involve skin, superficial and deep subcutaneous tissues, and any of the abdominopelvic fascial layers.

277
00:37:13,577 --> 00:37:18,502
In some cases, muscle also is involved—myofasciitis.

278
00:37:18,502 --> 00:37:24,889
Most of these necrotizing infections do not cause symptoms until 3 to 5 days after delivery.

279
00:37:24,889 --> 00:37:28,202
Some virulent infections develop earlier.

280
00:37:28,202 --> 00:37:36,952
Clinical findings vary, and it is frequently difficult to differentiate more innocuous superficial wound infections from a deep fascial one.

281
00:37:36,952 --> 00:37:42,177
If myofasciitis progresses, the woman may become ill from septicemia.

282
00:37:42,177 --> 00:37:49,289
A high index of suspicion, with surgical exploration if the diagnosis is uncertain, may be lifesaving .

283
00:37:49,289 --> 00:37:52,589
We aggressively pursue early exploration .

284
00:37:52,589 --> 00:38:10,352
Successful treatment of necrotizing soft-tissue infections involves early diagnosis, source control by surgical debridement, antimicrobials, and intensive care (Gallup, 2002; Goh, 2014; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2019).

285
00:38:10,352 --> 00:38:17,327
Surgery includes thorough debridement of all infected tissue, leaving wide margins of healthy bleeding tissue.

286
00:38:17,327 --> 00:38:24,689
This may include extensive abdominal or vulvar debridement and excision of abdominal, thigh, or buttock fascia.

287
00:38:24,689 --> 00:38:32,977
Death is virtually universal without surgical treatment, and rates approach 50 percent even if exhaustive debridement is performed .

288
00:38:32,977 --> 00:38:44,927
With substantial resection, synthetic mesh may ultimately be required later to close the fascial incision once infection is cleared (Gallup, 2002; McNeeley, 1998).

289
00:38:44,927 --> 00:38:48,839
# Peritonitis and Adnexal Abscesses.

290
00:38:48,839 --> 00:38:53,252
Following cesarean delivery, peritonitis is infrequent.

291
00:38:53,252 --> 00:39:00,814
It almost always is preceded by metritis, especially cases with uterine incisional necrosis and dehiscence.

292
00:39:00,814 --> 00:39:07,514
However, it may stem from a ruptured adnexal abscess or an inadvertent intraoperative bowel injury.

293
00:39:07,514 --> 00:39:11,677
Perforative appendicitis also can cause peritonitis .

294
00:39:11,677 --> 00:39:16,314
In these cases, prompt surgical treatment is usually indicated.

295
00:39:16,314 --> 00:39:22,877
After vaginal delivery, peritonitis is rarely encountered, and many such cases are due to

296
00:39:23,044 --> 00:39:28,706
virulent strains of group A β-hemolytic streptococci or similar organisms.

297
00:39:28,656 --> 00:39:37,944
Importantly, abdominal rigidity may not be prominent with puerperal peritonitis because of physiological abdominal wall laxity from pregnancy.

298
00:39:37,944 --> 00:39:44,894
Pain may be severe, but frequently, the first symptoms of peritonitis are those of adynamic ileus.

299
00:39:44,894 --> 00:39:50,156
Marked bowel distention may develop, which is unusual after vaginal birth.

300
00:39:50,156 --> 00:39:58,781
Normally, if the infection begins in an intact uterus and extends into the peritoneum, antimicrobial treatment alone suffices.

301
00:39:58,781 --> 00:40:02,869
An ovarian abscess rarely develops in the puerperium.

302
00:40:02,869 --> 00:40:08,644
These are presumably caused by bacterial invasion through an opening in the ovarian capsule .

303
00:40:08,644 --> 00:40:14,881
The abscess is usually unilateral, and women typically present 1 to 2 weeks after delivery.

304
00:40:14,881 --> 00:40:18,881
Rupture is common, and peritonitis may be severe.

305
00:40:18,881 --> 00:40:21,719
# Parametrial Phlegmon.

306
00:40:21,719 --> 00:40:33,869
For some women in whom metritis develops following cesarean delivery, parametrial cellulitis is intense and forms an area of induration—a phlegmon —within the leaves of the broad ligament .

307
00:40:33,869 --> 00:40:45,819
These infections are considered when fever persists longer than 72 hours despite intravenous antimicrobial therapy (Brown, 1999; DePalma, 1982).

308
00:40:45,819 --> 00:40:48,519
Figure 37-4.

309
00:40:48,519 --> 00:40:56,781
Left-sided parametrial phlegmon: cellulitis causes induration in the parametrium adjacent to the hysterotomy incision.

310
00:40:56,781 --> 00:41:02,094
Reproduced with permission from Worley KC: Postoperative complications.

311
00:41:02,094 --> 00:41:11,606
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

312
00:41:11,606 --> 00:41:16,094
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017).

313
00:41:16,094 --> 00:41:23,269
Phlegmons are usually unilateral, and they frequently are limited to the parametrium at the base of the broad ligament.

314
00:41:23,269 --> 00:41:30,081
If the inflammatory reaction is more intense, cellulitis extends along natural lines of cleavage.

315
00:41:30,081 --> 00:41:37,819
The most common form of extension is laterally along the broad ligament, with a tendency to extend to the pelvic sidewall.

316
00:41:37,819 --> 00:41:46,169
Occasionally, posterior extension may involve the rectovaginal septum, producing a firm mass posterior to the cervix.

317
00:41:46,169 --> 00:41:53,181
In most women, clinical improvement follows continued treatment with a broad-spectrum antimicrobial regimen.

318
00:41:53,181 --> 00:41:57,056
Typically, fever resolves in 5 to 7 days.

319
00:41:57,056 --> 00:42:03,319
Severe cellulitis of the uterine incision may ultimately lead to necrosis and separation .

320
00:42:03,319 --> 00:42:10,681
Extrusion of purulent material causes intraabdominal abscess formation and peritonitis as described above.

321
00:42:10,681 --> 00:42:16,006
Surgery is reserved for women in whom uterine incisional necrosis is suspected.

322
00:42:16,006 --> 00:42:20,294
For most, hysterectomy and surgical debridement are needed.

323
00:42:20,294 --> 00:42:29,606
These cases are difficult because the cervix and lower uterine segment are involved with an intense inflammatory process that extends to the pelvic sidewall.

324
00:42:29,606 --> 00:42:35,256
The adnexa are seldom involved, and one or both ovaries can usually be conserved.

325
00:42:35,256 --> 00:42:39,706
Blood loss is often appreciable, and transfusion is common.

326
00:42:39,706 --> 00:42:49,469
Imaging Studies Persistent puerperal infections can be evaluated using computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance (MR) imaging .

327
00:42:49,469 --> 00:42:59,856
Brown and associates (1991) used CT imaging in women in whom pelvic infection was refractory to antimicrobial therapy given for 5 days.

328
00:42:59,856 --> 00:43:08,006
They found at least one abnormal radiological finding in 75 percent of these women, and most were nonsurgical lesions.

329
00:43:08,006 --> 00:43:18,781
Fishel Bartal and colleagues (2018) reported abnormal CT findings in almost 60 percent of women with refractory fever persisting >3 days.

330
00:43:18,781 --> 00:43:25,906
Pelvic fluid collections were seen in 22 percent, and surgical intervention was prompted in 8 percent.

331
00:43:25,906 --> 00:43:31,481
Thus, imaging can be used to dissuade surgical exploration in most cases.

332
00:43:31,481 --> 00:43:39,481
Uterine incisional dehiscence such as shown in Figure 37-5 can sometimes be confirmed based on CT images.

333
00:43:39,481 --> 00:43:50,794
These findings must be interpreted within the clinical context because apparent uterine incisional defects thought to represent edema can be seen even after uncomplicated cesarean delivery.

334
00:43:50,794 --> 00:43:58,269
Shown in Figure 37-6 is a necrotic hysterotomy incision, which had leaked into the peritoneal cavity.

335
00:43:58,269 --> 00:44:00,994
Figure 37-5.

336
00:44:00,994 --> 00:44:08,206
Pelvic computed tomography scan showing necrosis of the uterine incision with gas in the myometrium (arrows).

337
00:44:08,206 --> 00:44:12,256
A large abscess (a) fills the right parametrium.

338
00:44:12,256 --> 00:44:15,069
Figure 37-6.

339
00:44:15,069 --> 00:44:17,356
Necrotic hysterotomy

340
00:44:17,524 --> 00:44:19,236
infection.

341
00:44:19,186 --> 00:44:26,536
Severe cellulitis of the uterine incision resulted in dehiscence with subsequent leakage into the peritoneal cavity.

342
00:44:26,536 --> 00:44:31,461
Hysterectomy was required for sufficient debridement of necrotic tissue.

343
00:44:31,461 --> 00:44:35,224
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Denisse Holcomb.

344
00:44:35,224 --> 00:44:43,999
Occasionally, a parametrial phlegmon may suppurate, forming a fluctuant broad ligament abscess that may point above the inguinal ligament.

345
00:44:43,999 --> 00:44:50,211
These abscesses can dissect anteriorly and be amenable to CT-directed needle drainage.

346
00:44:50,211 --> 00:45:02,486
A psoas abscess is rare, and despite antimicrobial therapy, percutaneous drainage may be required to effectively treat it (Shahabi, 2002; Swanson, 2008).

347
00:45:02,486 --> 00:45:09,874
If a phlegmon involving the rectovaginal septum suppurates, surgical drainage is easily effected by colpotomy.

348
00:45:09,874 --> 00:45:14,361
Absorption of the induration may require several days to weeks.

349
00:45:14,361 --> 00:45:17,849
# Septic Pelvic Thrombophlebitis.

350
00:45:17,849 --> 00:45:25,724
Suppurative thrombophlebitis was a frequent complication in the preantibiotic era, and septic embolization was common.

351
00:45:25,724 --> 00:45:34,299
However, with the advent of antimicrobial therapy, the mortality rate and need for surgical therapy for these infections diminished.

352
00:45:34,299 --> 00:45:40,424
Septic phlebitis arises as an extension along venous routes and may cause thrombosis.

353
00:45:40,424 --> 00:45:43,499
Lymphangitis often coexists.

354
00:45:43,499 --> 00:45:51,099
The ovarian veins may then become involved because they drain the upper uterus and therefore the placental implantation site.

355
00:45:51,099 --> 00:46:04,799
The experiences of Witlin and Sibai (1995) and Brown and coworkers (1999) suggest that puerperal septic thrombophlebitis is likely to involve one or both ovarian venous plexuses.

356
00:46:04,799 --> 00:46:11,499
In a fourth of women, the clot extends into the inferior vena cava and occasionally to the renal vein.

357
00:46:11,499 --> 00:46:15,811
The incidence of septic phlebitis varies in several reports.

358
00:46:15,811 --> 00:46:30,561
In a 5-year survey of 45,000 women who were delivered at Parkland Hospital, Brown and workers (1999) found an incidence of 1 case per 9000 vaginal births and 1 per 800 cesarean deliveries.

359
00:46:30,561 --> 00:46:43,061
In a cohort of 16,650 women undergoing primary cesarean delivery, Rouse and coworkers (2004) reported an incidence of 1 case per 400 surgeries.

360
00:46:43,061 --> 00:46:54,574
Incidences approximated 1 per 175 cesarean deliveries if chorioamnionitis was antecedent, but only 1 per 500 if there was no intrapartum infection.

361
00:46:54,574 --> 00:47:01,836
Except for chills and occasional lower quadrant pain, women with septic thrombophlebitis usually lack symptoms .

362
00:47:01,836 --> 00:47:06,761
The diagnosis can be confirmed by pelvic CT or MR imaging .

363
00:47:06,761 --> 00:47:21,336
Using either, Brown and coworkers (1999) found that 20 percent of 69 women with persistent fever following >5 days of antimicrobial therapy for metritis had septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.

364
00:47:21,336 --> 00:47:32,849
In a later study, Fischel Bartal and associates (2018) reported that 6 percent of women with refractory fever ≥3 days had septic phlebitis.

365
00:47:32,849 --> 00:47:40,211
These women normally have symptomatic improvement with antimicrobial treatment, however, they continue to have fever.

366
00:47:40,211 --> 00:47:42,936
Figure 37-7.

367
00:47:42,936 --> 00:47:53,661
