368
00:47:53,661 --> 00:47:57,136
Contrast is seen in ureter (white arrow).

369
00:47:57,136 --> 00:48:00,261
R = lower pole, right kidney.

370
00:48:00,261 --> 00:48:07,174
B. Coronal image demonstrates enlarged right ovarian vein filled with low-density thrombus (arrows).

371
00:48:07,174 --> 00:48:14,336
B: Reproduced with permission from Dr. April Bailey in Worley KC: Postoperative complications.

372
00:48:14,336 --> 00:48:23,849
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

373
00:48:23,849 --> 00:48:28,336
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

374
00:48:28,336 --> 00:48:31,274
Treatment with heparin is controversial.

375
00:48:31,274 --> 00:48:41,924
In a randomized study of 14 women, the addition of heparin to antimicrobial therapy for septic pelvic thrombophlebitis did not hasten recovery or improve outcome .

376
00:48:41,924 --> 00:48:46,149
We and others are of the opinion that heparin is unnecessary .

377
00:48:46,149 --> 00:48:54,486
Others, however, continue to recommend anticoagulation (Klima, 2008; Lenz, 2017).

378
00:48:54,486 --> 00:48:59,249
Certainly, no evidence supports long-term anticoagulation .

379
00:48:59,249 --> 00:49:02,124
# Perineal Infections.

380
00:49:02,124 --> 00:49:15,211
Episiotomy infections are uncommon, because the operation is now performed less frequently (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018a; Dillon, 2019).

381
00:49:15,211 --> 00:49:20,186
Reasons for this are discussed in Chapter 27 (p. 510).

382
00:49:20,186 --> 00:49:25,799
In an older study, Owen and Hauth (1990) described only 10

383
00:49:25,948 --> 00:49:30,760
episiotomy infections in 20,000 women delivered vaginally.

384
00:49:30,710 --> 00:49:34,598
With infection, however, dehiscence is a concern.

385
00:49:34,598 --> 00:49:45,048
Ramin and colleagues (1992) reported an episiotomy dehiscence rate of 0.5 percent at Parkland Hospital, and 80 percent of these were infected.

386
00:49:45,048 --> 00:49:52,660
Uygur and associates (2004) reported a 1-percent dehiscence rate and attributed two thirds to infection.

387
00:49:52,660 --> 00:50:06,460
When the anal sphincter is disrupted at delivery, the subsequent infection rate is higher and is likely influenced by intrapartum antimicrobial treatment (Buppasiri, 2014; Stock, 2013).

388
00:50:06,460 --> 00:50:13,560
Lewicky-Gaupp and colleagues (2015) reported a 20-percent infection rate when the sphincter was torn.

389
00:50:13,560 --> 00:50:24,110
Goldaber and coworkers (1993) described fourth-degree lacerations in 390 parturients, of whom 5.4 percent had morbidity.

390
00:50:24,110 --> 00:50:34,110
In these women, 2.8 percent had infection and dehiscence, 1.8 percent had only dehiscence, and 0.8 percent only infection.

391
00:50:34,110 --> 00:50:41,473
Pathogenesis and Clinical Course As noted, perineal laceration infection may be complicated by dehiscence.

392
00:50:41,473 --> 00:50:49,085
Other factors for separation include coagulation disorders, smoking, and human papillomavirus infection .

393
00:50:49,085 --> 00:50:53,410
No data suggest that dehiscence is related to faulty repair.

394
00:50:53,410 --> 00:51:00,335
With infection, local pain and dysuria, with or without urinary retention, are frequent symptoms.

395
00:51:00,335 --> 00:51:12,035
Ramin and colleagues (1992) reported that the most common findings were pain in 65 percent, purulent discharge in 65 percent, and fever in 44 percent.

396
00:51:12,035 --> 00:51:18,923
In extreme cases, the entire vulva may become edematous, ulcerated, and covered with exudate.

397
00:51:18,923 --> 00:51:25,885
Although life-threatening, septic shock or necrotizing fasciitis is rare (p. 654).

398
00:51:25,885 --> 00:51:32,073
Vaginal lacerations may also become infected directly or by extension from the perineum.

399
00:51:32,073 --> 00:51:37,160
The epithelium becomes red and swollen and may then become necrotic and slough.

400
00:51:37,160 --> 00:51:41,073
Parametrial extension can lead to lymphangitis.

401
00:51:41,073 --> 00:51:47,673
Cervical lacerations are seldom noticeably infected, but instead may manifest as metritis.

402
00:51:47,673 --> 00:51:57,535
Deep lacerations that extend directly into the base of the broad ligament may become infected and cause lymphangitis, parametritis, and bacteremia.

403
00:51:57,535 --> 00:52:03,548
Treatment Infected episiotomies are managed similar to other infected surgical wounds.

404
00:52:03,548 --> 00:52:12,935
In women with obvious cellulitis but no purulence, close observation and broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy alone may be appropriate.

405
00:52:12,935 --> 00:52:20,360
With purulence, drainage is established, and in most cases, sutures are removed and the infected wound debrided.

406
00:52:20,360 --> 00:52:25,873
With dehiscence, local wound care is coupled with intravenous antimicrobials.

407
00:52:25,873 --> 00:52:33,985
Hauth and associates (1986) were the first to advocate for early episiotomy repair after infection subsided.

408
00:52:33,985 --> 00:52:44,585
Hankins and colleagues (1990) described successful early repair in 94 percent of women, and the average duration from dehiscence to repair was 6 days.

409
00:52:44,585 --> 00:52:51,473
The two women with failures developed a pinpoint rectovaginal fistula that was treated with a small rectal flap.

410
00:52:51,473 --> 00:52:59,398
Other studies have shown similar high rates with early repair (Ramin, 1992; Uygur, 2004).

411
00:52:59,398 --> 00:53:04,235
Before performing early repair, diligent preparation is essential .

412
00:53:04,235 --> 00:53:08,810
The surgical wound must be properly cleaned and cleared of infection.

413
00:53:08,810 --> 00:53:16,723
Once the surface of the wound is free of exudate and covered by pink granulation tissue, secondary repair can be accomplished.

414
00:53:16,723 --> 00:53:24,223
The tissue must be adequately mobilized, with special attention to identify and mobilize the anal sphincter muscle.

415
00:53:24,223 --> 00:53:28,935
A tension-free suture line is essential to avoid repeated dehiscence.

416
00:53:28,935 --> 00:53:36,035
Secondary closure of the wound is accomplished in layers, as described for primary episiotomy closure .

417
00:53:36,035 --> 00:53:43,473
Postoperative care includes local wound care, stool softeners, and nothing per vagina or rectum until healed.

418
00:53:43,473 --> 00:53:50,135
Hard stools risk wound disruption, but liquid stool can seep between sutures to reincite infection.

419
00:53:50,135 --> 00:53:53,285
Thus, soft formed stools are the goal.

420
00:53:53,285 --> 00:53:55,935
Table 37-5.

421
00:53:55,935 --> 00:54:02,385
Preoperative Protocol for Early Repair of Episiotomy Dehiscence # Toxic Shock Syndrome.

422
00:54:02,385 --> 00:54:09,785
This acute febrile illness with severe multisystem derangement has a casefatality rate of 10 to 15 percent.

423
00:54:09,785 --> 00:54:21,048
Usual findings are fever, headache, mental confusion, diffuse macular erythematous rash, nausea, vomiting, watery diarrhea, and marked hemoconcentration.

424
00:54:21,048 --> 00:54:22,460
Renal

425
00:54:22,612 --> 00:54:31,899
failure followed by hepatic failure, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and circulatory collapse may progress in rapid sequence.

426
00:54:31,849 --> 00:54:35,812
During recovery, the rash-covered areas desquamate.

427
00:54:35,812 --> 00:54:44,887
In early investigations of toxic shock syndrome (TSS), Staphylococcus aureus was recovered from almost all afflicted persons.

428
00:54:44,887 --> 00:54:57,924
Specifically, a staphylococcal exotoxin, termed toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 (TSST-1), was found to cause the clinical manifestations by provoking profound endothelial injury.

429
00:54:57,924 --> 00:55:07,862
A very small amount of TSST-1 can activate T cells to create a “cytokine storm” (Heying, 2007; Que, 2005).

430
00:55:07,862 --> 00:55:12,224
Toxic shock syndrome has also been reported with MRSA .

431
00:55:12,224 --> 00:55:25,449
Subsequent investigations have also implicated virulent group A βhemolytic streptococcal infection (Anderson, 2014; Rottenstreich, 2019; Shinar, 2016).

432
00:55:25,449 --> 00:55:35,837
Heavy colonization or infection is complicated in some cases by streptococcal toxic shock syndrome, which is produced when pyrogenic exotoxin is elaborated.

433
00:55:35,837 --> 00:55:43,899
Serotypes M1 and M3 are particularly virulent (Beres, 2004; Okumura, 2004).

434
00:55:43,899 --> 00:55:56,299
Last, almost identical findings of toxic shock have been reported in pregnant women with Clostridium sordellii and novyi colonization (Herrera, 2016; Robbie, 2000).

435
00:55:56,299 --> 00:56:05,337
Thus, in some cases of toxic shock syndrome, infection is not apparent, and colonization of a mucosal surface is the presumed source .

436
00:56:05,337 --> 00:56:11,174
At least 10 to 20 percent of pregnant women have vaginal colonization with S aureus.

437
00:56:11,174 --> 00:56:17,799
And Clostridium perfringens and sordellii are cultured from 3 to 10 percent of asymptomatic women .

438
00:56:17,799 --> 00:56:28,874
Thus, it is not surprising that the disease develops in postpartum women when growth of vaginal bacteria is abundant (Chen, 2006; Guerinot, 1982).

439
00:56:28,874 --> 00:56:33,724
Delayed diagnosis and treatment are associated with maternal mortality .

440
00:56:33,724 --> 00:56:40,874
Crum and colleagues (2002) described a neonatal death following antenatal toxic shock syndrome.

441
00:56:40,874 --> 00:56:47,012
Principal therapy is supportive, while allowing reversal of capillary endothelial injury.

442
00:56:47,012 --> 00:56:53,449
Antimicrobial therapy includes coverage against staphylococcal and streptococcal species.

443
00:56:53,449 --> 00:57:01,674
With evidence of pelvic infection, antimicrobial therapy must also include agents used for polymicrobial infections.

444
00:57:01,674 --> 00:57:07,224
Women with these infections may require wound debridement and possibly hysterectomy.

445
00:57:07,224 --> 00:57:12,587
Because the toxin is so potent, the mortality rate is correspondingly high .

446
00:57:12,587 --> 00:57:14,624
Breast Infections.

447
00:57:14,624 --> 00:57:23,899
Parenchymal infection of the mammary glands is a rare antepartum complication, but the postpartum incidence of mastitis approximates 3 percent .

448
00:57:23,899 --> 00:57:29,199
No evidence supports use of prophylactic measures to prevent breast infection .

449
00:57:29,199 --> 00:57:40,087
Risk factors include nursing difficulties, cracked nipples, and oral antibiotic therapy (Branch-Elliman, 2012; Mediano, 2014).

450
00:57:40,087 --> 00:57:48,612
Symptoms of suppurative mastitis seldom appear before the end of the first week postpartum and usually are not seen until the third or fourth week.

451
00:57:48,612 --> 00:57:55,262
Infection almost invariably is unilateral, and marked engorgement usually precedes inflammation.

452
00:57:55,262 --> 00:58:01,524
Symptoms include chills or actual rigors, which are soon followed by fever and tachycardia.

453
00:58:01,524 --> 00:58:05,449
Pain is severe, and the breast(s) becomes hard and red.

454
00:58:05,449 --> 00:58:10,337
Approximately 10 percent of women with mastitis develop an abscess.

455
00:58:10,337 --> 00:58:16,212
Detection of fluctuation may be difficult, and sonography is usually diagnostic .

456
00:58:16,212 --> 00:58:26,449
Although rare, toxic shock syndrome from mastitis caused by S aureus has been reported (Demey, 1989; Fujiwara, 2001).

457
00:58:26,449 --> 00:58:29,012
Figure 37-8.

458
00:58:29,012 --> 00:58:32,262
Puerperal mastitis with breast abscess.

459
00:58:32,262 --> 00:58:38,299
A. Indurated, erythematous skin overlies the area of right-sided breast infection.

460
00:58:38,299 --> 00:58:42,637
B. Sonographic picture of this 5-cm abscess.

461
00:58:42,637 --> 00:58:44,949
# Etiology.

462
00:58:44,949 --> 00:58:51,937
S aureus, especially MRSA, is the most commonly isolated organism in breast infections.

463
00:58:51,937 --> 00:58:58,424
Matheson and coworkers (1988) found it in 40 percent of women with mastitis.

464
00:58:58,424 --> 00:59:05,724
Other commonly isolated organisms are coagulasenegative staphylococci and viridans streptococci.

465
00:59:05,724 --> 00:59:11,949
The immediate source of mastitis-causing organisms is almost always the newborn’s nose and throat.

466
00:59:11,949 --> 00:59:16,762
Bacteria enter the breast through the nipple at fissures or small abrasions.

467
00:59:16,762 --> 00:59:20,812
The infecting organism can usually be cultured from milk.

468
00:59:20,812 --> 00:59:25,974
At times, suppurative mastitis reaches epidemic levels among nursing

469
00:59:26,140 --> 00:59:27,727
mothers.

470
00:59:27,677 --> 00:59:34,915
Such outbreaks most often coincide with the appearance of a new strain of antibiotic-resistant staphylococcus.

471
00:59:34,915 --> 00:59:47,652
A contemporaneous example is MRSA, which has rapidly become the most commonly isolated staphylococcal species in some areas (Berens, 2010; Klevens, 2007).

472
00:59:47,652 --> 01:00:01,002
At Parkland Hospital from 2000 to 2004, Laibl and associates (2005) reported that a fourth of community-acquired MRSA isolates were from pregnant or postpartum women with mastitis.

473
01:00:01,002 --> 01:00:09,815
Hospital-acquired MRSA may cause mastitis when the newborn becomes colonized after contact with nursery personnel who are colonized .

474
01:00:09,815 --> 01:00:18,440
Stafford and colleagues (2008) found a higher incidence of recurrent abscess in those with MRSA-associated mastitis.

475
01:00:18,440 --> 01:00:20,665
# Management.

476
01:00:20,665 --> 01:00:29,702
Provided that appropriate therapy for mastitis is started before suppuration begins, the infection usually resolves within 48 hours.

477
01:00:29,702 --> 01:00:36,527
Many recommend that milk be expressed from the affected breast onto a swab and cultured before beginning therapy.

478
01:00:36,527 --> 01:00:46,015
Bacterial identification and antimicrobial sensitivities can provide information for a successful program of nosocomial infection surveillance .

479
01:00:46,015 --> 01:00:49,665
The most effective treatment has not been clarified .

480
01:00:49,665 --> 01:00:58,340
Thus, the initial antimicrobial choice is influenced by current experience with staphylococcal infections at a given institution.

481
01:00:58,340 --> 01:01:04,777
Dicloxacillin, 500 mg orally four times daily, may be started empirically.

482
01:01:04,777 --> 01:01:09,152
Erythromycin is given to women who are penicillin sensitive.

483
01:01:09,152 --> 01:01:24,227
If the infection is caused by resistant, penicillinase-producing staphylococcus species or if resistant organisms are suspected while awaiting the culture results, then vancomycin, clindamycin, or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is given .

484
01:01:24,227 --> 01:01:29,690
Although clinical response may be prompt, treatment is recommended for 10 days.

485
01:01:29,690 --> 01:01:36,215
Marshall and coworkers (1975) demonstrated the importance of continued breastfeeding.

486
01:01:36,215 --> 01:01:45,002
They reported that of 65 women with mastitis, the only three who developed abscesses were among the 15 women who quit breastfeeding.

487
01:01:45,002 --> 01:01:49,027
Vigorous milk expression may be sufficient treatment alone .

488
01:01:49,027 --> 01:01:53,027
Sometimes the infant will not nurse on the inflamed breast.

489
01:01:53,027 --> 01:02:01,840
This probably is not related to any changes in the milk taste but is secondary to engorgement and edema, which can make the areola harder to grip.

490
01:02:01,840 --> 01:02:04,277
Pumping can alleviate this.

491
01:02:04,277 --> 01:02:09,615
When nursing bilaterally, it is best to begin suckling on the uninvolved breast.

492
01:02:09,615 --> 01:02:14,015
This allows let-down to commence before moving to the tender breast.

493
01:02:14,015 --> 01:02:22,790
In resource-poor countries, breastfeeding in women infected with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is not contraindicated.

494
01:02:22,790 --> 01:02:30,252
However, in the setting of mastitis or breast abscess, it is recommended to stop feeding from the infected breast.

495
01:02:30,252 --> 01:02:35,227
This is because HIV RNA levels rise in affected breast milk.

496
01:02:35,227 --> 01:02:39,290
These levels return to baseline after symptoms resolve .

497
01:02:39,290 --> 01:02:41,727
# Breast Abscess.

498
01:02:41,727 --> 01:02:50,465
In a population-based study of nearly 1.5 million Swedish women, the incidence of breast abscess was 0.1 percent .

499
01:02:50,465 --> 01:02:58,840
An abscess should be suspected when defervescence does not follow within 48 to 72 hours of treatment or when a mass is palpable.

500
01:02:58,840 --> 01:03:02,365
Again, sonographic imaging is valuable.

501
01:03:02,365 --> 01:03:08,215
Breast abscesses can be large, and in one case report, 2 L of pus were released .

502
01:03:08,215 --> 01:03:14,302
Traditional therapy has been surgical drainage, which usually requires general anesthesia.

503
01:03:14,302 --> 01:03:19,652
The incision ideally is placed along Langer skin lines for a cosmetic result .

504
01:03:19,652 --> 01:03:26,515
In early cases, a single incision over the most dependent portion of fluctuation is usually sufficient.

505
01:03:26,515 --> 01:03:32,102
Multiple abscesses require several incisions and disruption of loculations.

506
01:03:32,102 --> 01:03:39,652
The resulting cavity is loosely packed with gauze, which should be replaced at the end of 24 hours by a smaller pack.

507
01:03:39,652 --> 01:03:46,752
More recently, sonographically guided needle aspiration using local analgesia has become favored .

508
01:03:46,752 --> 01:03:50,452
This has an 80- to 90-percent success rate .

509
01:03:50,452 --> 01:03:57,890
In a randomized trial, Naeem and colleagues (2012) compared surgical drainage with aspiration.

510
01:03:57,890 --> 01:04:06,615
At 8 weeks, they found 93 percent undergoing aspiration were healed compared with 77 percent undergoing surgical drainage.

511
01:04:06,615 --> 01:04:13,640
Sonographic findings, initial choice of antimicrobials, and infecting organism do not predict

512
01:04:13,804 --> 01:04:16,041
aspiration failure .

513
01:04:15,991 --> 01:04:20,891
Other etiologies should be considered in the setting of a nonhealing abscess.

514
01:04:20,891 --> 01:04:29,629
Rarely, granulomatous mastitis presents as puerperal mastitis (Ding, 2021 Freeman, 2017).

515
01:04:29,629 --> 01:04:33,529
Cancer or tuberculosis are other considerations .

